See also: @Contents

= Programs, lines and comments
A program is plain text, one statement or more a line, separated by ":". A
line may start with a number (a V2-style line number) or a label ("Start:").
Names have up to 32 letters, digits and "_", all significant, in any case;
"%" at the end makes an integer, "$" a string, no suffix a float (or an
integer after OPTION INTEGER).

@REM @LET @END @STOP @CONT @OPTION @INTEGER @MEMORY @DEFINT

= REM
REM text
' text

A comment: the rest of the line is kept as it is and ignored. The apostrophe
is the short form and may follow a statement.

  REM Bird Watch, version 1
  count% = 0   ' birds so far

See also: @DATA

= LET
[LET] variable = expression

Gives a variable a value. The word LET may be left out.

  LET a% = 1
  name$ = "Robin"

See also: @CONST @DIM

= END
END

Ends the program: files are closed, voices silenced, and the program quits
(or returns to the editor).

  IF ASK("Quit now?") THEN END

See also: @STOP @{END IF} @{END SUB}

= STOP
STOP

Stops the program as if Ctrl+C was pressed: under the editor it breaks into
the debugger at this line; CONT continues. Also the event word of ON STOP
and the timer state of TIMER n STOP.

  IF total% < 0 THEN STOP

See also: @CONT @END @ON

= CONT
CONT

Continues a program stopped by STOP, Ctrl+C or a breakpoint. Used from the
editor's Run menu; in a program it does nothing.

  CONT

See also: @STOP

= OPTION
OPTION INTEGER
OPTION MEMORY banks

OPTION INTEGER makes names without a suffix 16-bit integers instead of
floats: faster, and what new programs should start with. OPTION MEMORY gives
the variables, arrays and strings that many 8 KB RAM banks (4 to 24; 4
without it).

  OPTION INTEGER
  OPTION MEMORY 8

See also: @DEFINT @INTEGER @MEMORY

= INTEGER
OPTION INTEGER

The word of OPTION INTEGER: unsuffixed names are integers.

  OPTION INTEGER

See also: @OPTION @DEFINT

= MEMORY
OPTION MEMORY banks

The word of OPTION MEMORY: how many RAM banks the variables, arrays and
strings get.

  OPTION MEMORY 12

See also: @OPTION @FRE

= DEFINT
DEFINT letter[-letter] [, ...]

Names without a suffix whose first letter is in the ranges are integers (as
V2 programs from other BASICs write it). DEFINT A-Z is OPTION INTEGER.

  DEFINT A-Z
  DEFINT I-N, X

See also: @OPTION

= The console: PRINT and INPUT
PRINT and INPUT use the console, a window of 8 x 8 cards that opens on the
first PRINT and scrolls. A V2 listing runs in it unchanged. With #h they use
an open file instead.

@PRINT @INPUT @{LINE INPUT} @GET @TAB @SPC

= PRINT
PRINT [#h,] [value] [{; | ,} value] ... [; | ,]

Writes values to the console, or to file h. ";" joins values without a gap,
"," moves to the next 10-column zone; a ";" or "," at the end keeps the line
open. "?" is short for PRINT.

  PRINT "Hello"
  PRINT "Birds:"; count%, "Time: "; TIME$
  PRINT #1, name$

See also: @INPUT @TAB @SPC @OPEN

= INPUT
INPUT [#h,] ["prompt";] variable [, variable] ...

Reads values typed in the console (with a "?" prompt) or read from file h,
separated by commas, into the variables.

  INPUT "Your name"; name$
  INPUT #1, x, y

See also: @{LINE INPUT} @GET @INPUTBOX$

= LINE INPUT
LINE INPUT [#h,] ["prompt";] string-variable

Reads a whole line, commas and quotes included, from the console or from
file h.

  LINE INPUT "Note: "; note$
  LINE INPUT #1, first$

See also: @INPUT @EOF

= GET
GET string-variable
GET #h, string-variable, count

Without #: the next key from the queue, "" when none (it does not wait).
With #h: up to count bytes from file h.

  GET k$
  GET #1, block$, 128

See also: @INKEY$ @PUT @INPUT

= TAB
TAB(column)

In PRINT: moves to the column (0 is the left edge).

  PRINT "Name"; TAB(20); "Count"

See also: @SPC @PRINT

= SPC
SPC(count)

In PRINT: writes count spaces.

  PRINT "A"; SPC(5); "B"

See also: @TAB @PRINT

= Jumps and decisions
A line number or a label is a jump target. IF has a one-line form and a
block form: THEN at the end of the line starts a block that END IF closes.

@GOTO @GOSUB @RETURN @ON @IF @THEN @ELSE @ELSEIF @{END IF}

= GOTO
GOTO line
GOTO label

Continues at the line number or the label. The first jump to a number looks
it up; later ones use the cached place.

  GOTO 100
  GOTO Start

See also: @GOSUB @ON @IF

= GOSUB
GOSUB line
GOSUB label

Calls the lines at the target as a subroutine; RETURN comes back to the
statement after the GOSUB. New programs use SUB instead.

  GOSUB 900
  GOSUB DrawBoard

See also: @RETURN @SUB @ON

= RETURN
RETURN
RETURN expression (in a FUNCTION)

Without a value: back from a GOSUB. With one, in a FUNCTION: ends the
function with that value.

  RETURN
  RETURN w * h

See also: @GOSUB @FUNCTION

= ON
ON n GOTO|GOSUB targets
ON event CALL procedure

ON n GOTO 10, 20, 30 jumps to the n-th target (none when n is out of range).
ON ERROR GOTO line, ON ERROR GOTO 0 and ON STOP are kept for V2-style
programs. ON CLICK, KEY, WHEEL, CLOSE #w and QUIT bind an event to a SUB
(the event model).

  ON choice% GOTO 100, 200, 300
  ON ERROR GOTO 900
  ON CLICK CALL Clicked
  ON CLOSE #1 CALL DoQuit

See also: @GOTO @CALL @CLICK @ERROR

= IF
IF condition THEN statements [ELSE statements]
IF condition THEN ... [ELSEIF ...] [ELSE ...] END IF

Runs statements when the condition is not 0. With THEN at the end of the
line it starts a block that ends with END IF, with ELSEIF and ELSE parts.
THEN followed by a line number jumps there.

  IF x% > 10 THEN x% = 0
  IF a$ = "" THEN 200 ELSE PRINT a$
  IF score% > best% THEN
    best% = score%
  ELSE
    PRINT "Try again"
  END IF

See also: @THEN @ELSE @ELSEIF @{END IF} @{SELECT CASE}

= THEN
IF condition THEN ...

Separates IF's condition from what it runs. At the end of a line it starts
an IF block.

  IF k$ = "q" THEN END

See also: @IF @ELSE

= ELSE
IF ... THEN ... ELSE ...
ELSE (in an IF block)

What IF runs when the condition is 0: on the same line in the one- line
form, or the lines up to END IF in a block.

  IF n% = 1 THEN PRINT "bird" ELSE PRINT "birds"

See also: @IF @ELSEIF @{END IF}

= ELSEIF
ELSEIF condition THEN

Inside an IF block: a further condition, tested when the ones before it were
0.

  IF t < 0 THEN
    PRINT "frost"
  ELSEIF t < 10 THEN
    PRINT "cold"
  END IF

See also: @IF @ELSE @{END IF}

= END IF
END IF

Ends an IF block.

  IF ok% THEN
    PRINT "Saved"
  END IF

See also: @IF

= Loops
FOR counts; WHILE tests first, REPEAT and DO ... LOOP UNTIL test last. EXIT
leaves the innermost loop of its kind. An integer FOR (FOR i% = ...) never
touches the float code.

@FOR @TO @STEP @NEXT @WHILE @WEND @REPEAT @UNTIL @DO @LOOP @EXIT @{EXIT FOR}
@{EXIT WHILE} @{EXIT DO} @{EXIT REPEAT}

= FOR
FOR variable = first TO last [STEP step]

Runs the lines up to NEXT with the variable counting from first to last (by
step, 1 if none; a negative step counts down).

  FOR i% = 1 TO 10
    PRINT i%
  NEXT
  FOR x = 1 TO 0 STEP -0.25 : PRINT x : NEXT x

See also: @NEXT @TO @STEP @{EXIT FOR}

= TO
FOR v = a TO b   CASE a TO b   COPY ... TO x, y

The range word of FOR and CASE, and the target of COPY.

  FOR i% = 0 TO 79 : NEXT

See also: @FOR @CASE @COPY

= STEP
FOR v = a TO b STEP s

The amount FOR adds to its variable each time (1 without STEP).

  FOR y% = 470 TO 0 STEP -10 : NEXT

See also: @FOR

= NEXT
NEXT [variable [, variable] ...]

Ends a FOR loop. With names it ends those loops, innermost first.

  FOR i% = 1 TO 3 : FOR j% = 1 TO 3 : PRINT i% * j% : NEXT j%, i%

See also: @FOR

= WHILE
WHILE condition ... WEND

Runs the lines up to WEND as long as the condition is not 0; tested before
each pass.

  f$ = DIR$("*.TXT")
  WHILE f$ <> ""
    PRINT f$ : f$ = DIR$()
  WEND

See also: @WEND @{EXIT WHILE} @DO

= WEND
WEND

Ends a WHILE loop.

  WHILE n% > 0 : n% = n% \ 2 : WEND

See also: @WHILE

= REPEAT
REPEAT ... UNTIL condition

Runs the lines up to UNTIL, then again while the condition is 0; at least
once.

  REPEAT
    k$ = INKEY$
  UNTIL k$ <> ""

See also: @UNTIL @DO

= UNTIL
UNTIL condition
LOOP UNTIL condition

Ends a REPEAT loop; also the test word of DO ... LOOP.

  REPEAT : n% = n% + 1 : UNTIL n% = 10

See also: @REPEAT @LOOP

= DO
DO [WHILE|UNTIL condition] ... LOOP [WHILE|UNTIL condition]

A loop with its test at the top, at the bottom, or none (left with EXIT DO).

  DO
    DOEVENTS
    IF INKEY$ = "q" THEN EXIT DO
  LOOP

See also: @LOOP @{EXIT DO} @WHILE

= LOOP
LOOP [WHILE|UNTIL condition]

Ends a DO loop.

  DO : i% = i% + 1 : LOOP UNTIL i% = 5

See also: @DO

= EXIT
EXIT FOR|WHILE|DO|REPEAT|SUB|FUNCTION

The first word of the EXIT statements: each leaves the innermost block of
its kind.

  EXIT FOR

See also: @{EXIT FOR} @{EXIT SUB}

= EXIT FOR
EXIT FOR

Leaves the innermost FOR loop, past its NEXT.

  FOR i% = 0 TO 99 : IF a%(i%) = 0 THEN EXIT FOR
  NEXT

See also: @FOR @EXIT

= EXIT WHILE
EXIT WHILE

Leaves the innermost WHILE loop, past its WEND.

  WHILE 1 : IF EOF(1) THEN EXIT WHILE
  WEND

See also: @WHILE @EXIT

= EXIT DO
EXIT DO

Leaves the innermost DO loop, past its LOOP.

  DO : IF MOUSEB THEN EXIT DO
  LOOP

See also: @DO @EXIT

= EXIT REPEAT
EXIT REPEAT

Leaves the innermost REPEAT loop, past its UNTIL.

  REPEAT : IF k$ = "x" THEN EXIT REPEAT
  UNTIL 0

See also: @REPEAT @EXIT

= Choosing: SELECT CASE
SELECT CASE compares one value with a list of cases and runs the first that
matches.

@SELECT @{SELECT CASE} @CASE @{CASE ELSE} @IS @{END SELECT}

= SELECT
SELECT CASE expression

The first word of SELECT CASE. In an expression it is the colour number 4
(the selection colour) of PAPER.

  SELECT CASE n%
  END SELECT

See also: @{SELECT CASE} @PAPER

= SELECT CASE
SELECT CASE expression ... END SELECT

Starts a choice between CASE parts: the first CASE whose list holds the
value runs, or CASE ELSE when none does.

  SELECT CASE k$
    CASE "a", "e", "i", "o", "u"
      PRINT "vowel"
    CASE ELSE
      PRINT "other"
  END SELECT

See also: @CASE @{CASE ELSE} @{END SELECT} @IF

= CASE
CASE value [TO value] | IS op value [, ...]

One part of SELECT CASE: values, ranges (1 TO 5) and comparisons (IS > 9),
separated by commas.

  SELECT CASE n%
    CASE 1, 2 TO 5, IS > 9
      PRINT "yes"
  END SELECT

See also: @{SELECT CASE} @IS @TO

= CASE ELSE
CASE ELSE

The part of SELECT CASE that runs when no CASE matched; the last.

  SELECT CASE d%
    CASE 0 : PRINT "none"
    CASE ELSE : PRINT d%
  END SELECT

See also: @{SELECT CASE} @CASE

= IS
CASE IS op value

In CASE: compares the SELECT value with = <> < > <= or >=.

  SELECT CASE t : CASE IS < 0 : PRINT "frost" : END SELECT

See also: @CASE

= END SELECT
END SELECT

Ends a SELECT CASE.

  SELECT CASE x% : CASE 1 : PRINT "one" : END SELECT

See also: @{SELECT CASE}

= Procedures
SUB and FUNCTION make procedures with their own parameters and local
variables; they may call themselves. A SUB is called by its name and its
arguments, or with CALL. Scalars are passed by value, arrays (a%()) and
BYREF parameters by reference.

@SUB @{END SUB} @FUNCTION @{END FUNCTION} @{EXIT SUB} @{EXIT FUNCTION} @CALL
@BYREF @LOCAL @SHARED

= SUB
SUB Name[(parameter, ...)] ... END SUB

Defines a procedure. Its parameters are local. A SUB is also what menu
items, icons, timers and events call.

  SUB Greet(who$)
    PRINT "Hello, "; who$
  END SUB
  Greet "Robin"

See also: @{END SUB} @CALL @FUNCTION @LOCAL @{EXIT SUB}

= END SUB
END SUB

Ends a SUB; the call returns.

  SUB Tick : WINDOW STATUS #1, "", TIME$ : END SUB

See also: @SUB

= FUNCTION
FUNCTION Name[(parameter, ...)] ... END FUNCTION

Defines a function: a procedure with a value. Assign the value to the
function's name, or give it with RETURN. The name's suffix is the value's
type.

  FUNCTION Area(w, h)
    Area = w * h
  END FUNCTION
  PRINT Area(3, 4)

See also: @{END FUNCTION} @RETURN @SUB

= END FUNCTION
END FUNCTION

Ends a FUNCTION; the call returns its value.

  FUNCTION Twice%(n%) : Twice% = 2 * n% : END FUNCTION

See also: @FUNCTION

= EXIT SUB
EXIT SUB

Returns from a SUB at once.

  SUB Save : IF f$ = "" THEN EXIT SUB
  END SUB

See also: @SUB @{EXIT FUNCTION}

= EXIT FUNCTION
EXIT FUNCTION

Returns from a FUNCTION at once with the value given so far.

  FUNCTION Find%(k$) : Find% = -1 : IF k$ = "" THEN EXIT FUNCTION
  END FUNCTION

See also: @FUNCTION @RETURN

= CALL
CALL Name[(argument, ...)]

Calls a SUB. The same as writing the name and its arguments; also the word
that binds events and timers to a SUB.

  CALL Greet("Robin")
  TIMER 1, 60 CALL Tick

See also: @SUB @ON @TIMER

= BYREF
SUB Name(BYREF parameter)

A parameter passed by reference: assigning it changes the caller's variable.

  SUB Swap(BYREF a%, BYREF b%)
    LOCAL t% : t% = a% : a% = b% : b% = t%
  END SUB

See also: @SUB @FUNCTION

= LOCAL
LOCAL name [, name] ...

Inside a procedure: variables of its own, new at each call (so recursion
works), gone at its end.

  SUB Count : LOCAL i%, s$
  END SUB

See also: @SHARED @SUB

= SHARED
SHARED name [, name] ...

Inside a procedure: says the names are the program's globals. A name a
procedure does not make LOCAL is global anyway; SHARED says so on purpose.

  SUB Tick : SHARED ticks% : ticks% = ticks% + 1 : END SUB

See also: @LOCAL

= Data, constants and arrays
@DATA @READ @RESTORE @CONST @DIM @REDIM @UBOUND

= DATA
DATA item [, item] ...

Values for READ, kept as written (numbers, or text in quotes when it holds
commas). In an expression (FOLDER$, PICTURE$) DATA is the number 5.

  DATA 3, "Robin", "Wren", "Blue tit"

See also: @READ @RESTORE

= READ
READ variable [, variable] ...

Takes the next DATA items into the variables.

  READ n% : FOR i% = 1 TO n% : READ b$ : PRINT b$ : NEXT

See also: @DATA @RESTORE

= RESTORE
RESTORE [line | label]

The next READ starts at the first DATA again, or at the first DATA at or
after the target.

  RESTORE
  RESTORE Birds

See also: @READ @DATA

= CONST
CONST name = value

A name for a value that never changes; assigning it is an error.

  CONST W% = 640, H% = 480

See also: @LET

= DIM
DIM name(size [, size [, size]]) [, ...]

Makes arrays of up to 3 dimensions, indices from 0 to size. They live in RAM
banks and may be large.

  DIM a%(100), grid$(10, 10)

See also: @REDIM @UBOUND

= REDIM
REDIM name(size [, ...]) [, ...]

Makes an array again with new sizes; its old values are gone.

  REDIM list$(n% * 2)

See also: @DIM

= UBOUND
UBOUND(array [, dimension])

The largest index of the array's dimension (1 if none is given).

  FOR i% = 0 TO UBOUND(a%) : a%(i%) = 0 : NEXT

See also: @DIM

= Errors
An error stops the program unless TRY catches it. ERR, ERR$ and ERL say what
happened and where.

@TRY @CATCH @{END TRY} @ERROR @RESUME @ERR @ERR$ @ERL @ERRFILE

= TRY
TRY ... CATCH ... END TRY

Runs the lines up to CATCH; an error there runs the lines from CATCH to END
TRY instead of stopping the program.

  TRY
    OPEN #1, "Bird list.txt", "r"
  CATCH
    MSGBOX "Could not read the list: " + ERR$
  END TRY

See also: @CATCH @{END TRY} @ERR @ERROR

= CATCH
CATCH

Where a TRY block continues after an error.

  TRY : KILL f$ : CATCH : PRINT "Not there" : END TRY

See also: @TRY

= END TRY
END TRY

Ends a TRY block.

  TRY : x = 1 / y : CATCH : x = 0 : END TRY

See also: @TRY

= ERROR
ERROR number [, message]

Raises an error. 200-255 are free for programs. Also the word of ON ERROR
GOTO.

  ERROR 200, "No birds"
  ON ERROR GOTO 900

See also: @TRY @ERR @ON

= RESUME
RESUME [NEXT | line | label]

From an ON ERROR GOTO handler: runs the failing statement again, the one
after it (NEXT), or continues at a target.

  900 PRINT "Error"; ERR : RESUME NEXT

See also: @ON @ERROR

= ERR
ERR

The number of the last error.

  TRY : OPEN #1, f$, "r" : CATCH : PRINT ERR : END TRY

See also: @ERR$ @ERL @ERRFILE

= ERR$
ERR$

The message of the last error.

  PRINT "Failed: "; ERR$

See also: @ERR @ERL

= ERL
ERL

The line of the last error: its line number, or the source line.

  PRINT "in line"; ERL

See also: @ERR

= ERRFILE
ERRFILE

The kernel's error code (its x register) of the last file or system error; 0
for other errors.

  IF ERRFILE = 5 THEN PRINT "no such file"

See also: @ERR @OPEN

= Operators
From the highest priority: ^; unary -; * / \ MOD; + -; SHL SHR; comparisons;
NOT; AND; OR XOR. AND, OR, XOR and NOT work on the bits of integers; a
comparison gives -1 (true) or 0.

@AND @OR @XOR @NOT @MOD @SHL @SHR @{<=} @{<>} @{>=}

= AND
a AND b

Both true; on integers, the bits set in both.

  IF MOUSEB AND 1 THEN PRINT "left"

See also: @OR @XOR @NOT

= OR
a OR b

Either true; on integers, the bits set in either.

  flags% = flags% OR 4

See also: @AND @XOR

= XOR
a XOR b

One true but not both; on integers, the bits that differ.

  v% = v% XOR $FF

See also: @AND @OR

= NOT
NOT a

True when a is false; on integers, every bit turned over.

  IF NOT done% THEN PRINT "busy"

See also: @AND

= MOD
a MOD b

The remainder of a divided by b.

  IF n% MOD 2 = 0 THEN PRINT "even"

See also: @SHL

= SHL
a SHL n

The integer a with its bits moved n places left.

  hi% = b% SHL 8

See also: @SHR

= SHR
a SHR n

The integer a with its bits moved n places right.

  lo% = w% SHR 8

See also: @SHL

= <=
a <= b

True when a is less than or equal to b (numbers or strings).

  IF x% <= 0 THEN x% = 0

See also: @{>=} @{<>}

= <>
a <> b

True when a and b differ.

  WHILE k$ <> "q" : k$ = INKEY$ : WEND

See also: @{<=} @{>=}

= >=
a >= b

True when a is greater than or equal to b.

  IF y% >= 480 THEN y% = 479

See also: @{<=} @{<>}

= Strings
Strings hold up to 255 Latin-1 characters; + joins them. The first character
is position 1.

@LEFT$ @MID$ @RIGHT$ @LEN @STR$ @VAL @CHR$ @ASC

= LEFT$
LEFT$(s$, n)

The first n characters of s$.

  PRINT LEFT$("Robin", 3)

See also: @RIGHT$ @MID$

= MID$
MID$(s$, start [, length])

The characters of s$ from start on (length of them, or all).

  PRINT MID$("Blackbird", 6)

See also: @LEFT$ @RIGHT$

= RIGHT$
RIGHT$(s$, n)

The last n characters of s$.

  ext$ = RIGHT$(f$, 3)

See also: @LEFT$ @MID$

= LEN
LEN(s$)

The number of characters of s$.

  PRINT LEN("Wren")

See also: @MID$

= STR$
STR$(number)

A number as text (with a space before a positive number, as V2).

  t$ = "Count:" + STR$(count%)

See also: @VAL

= VAL
VAL(s$)

The number at the start of s$ (0 if none).

  n% = VAL(INPUTBOX$("How many?"))

See also: @STR$

= CHR$
CHR$(code)

The character with the Latin-1 code.

  q$ = CHR$(34)

See also: @ASC

= ASC
ASC(s$)

The Latin-1 code of the first character of s$.

  PRINT ASC("A")

See also: @CHR$

= Numbers
Floats use the X16 ROM's maths library (the V2 5-byte format, about 9
digits). Integer arithmetic stays 16-bit.

@INT @ABS @SGN @SQR @SIN @COS @TAN @ATN @EXP @LOG @RND

= INT
INT(x)

The largest whole number not above x.

  PRINT INT(-2.5)

See also: @ABS @SGN

= ABS
ABS(x)

x without its sign.

  d% = ABS(x1% - x2%)

See also: @SGN

= SGN
SGN(x)

-1, 0 or 1 by the sign of x.

  dx% = SGN(tx% - x%)

See also: @ABS

= SQR
SQR(x)

The square root of x.

  r = SQR(x * x + y * y)

See also: @EXP

= SIN
SIN(x)

The sine of x (radians).

  y% = 240 + 100 * SIN(a)

See also: @COS @TAN @ATN

= COS
COS(x)

The cosine of x (radians).

  x% = 320 + 100 * COS(a)

See also: @SIN

= TAN
TAN(x)

The tangent of x (radians).

  PRINT TAN(0.5)

See also: @SIN @ATN

= ATN
ATN(x)

The arc tangent of x, in radians.

  pi = 4 * ATN(1)

See also: @TAN

= EXP
EXP(x)

e to the power x.

  PRINT EXP(1)

See also: @LOG

= LOG
LOG(x)

The natural logarithm of x (x > 0).

  PRINT LOG(10) / LOG(2)

See also: @EXP

= RND
RND(x)

A random number from 0 up to (not including) 1. A negative x starts a new
sequence from it.

  d% = INT(RND(1) * 6) + 1

See also: @INT

= Memory and the machine
@PEEK @POKE @SYS @FRE @TI @TI$ @TRON @TROFF

= PEEK
PEEK(address)

The byte at a main RAM address.

  PRINT PEEK($0801)

See also: @POKE

= POKE
POKE address, byte

Writes a byte to main RAM. There is no text screen at $0400; keep below
$A000 and out of the kernel's areas.

  POKE $6000, 255

See also: @PEEK @SYS

= SYS
SYS address

Calls machine code at an address below $A000; it must end with RTS and leave
the zero page from $50 as it found it.

  SYS $6000

See also: @POKE

= FRE
FRE(0)

The free bytes of the variables' banks.

  PRINT FRE(0); "bytes free"

See also: @OPTION

= TI
TI

Frames (1/60 s) since the program started.

  t = TI : SLEEP 30 : PRINT TI - t

See also: @TI$ @TIME$ @SLEEP

= TI$
TI$

The clock as "HHMMSS", as V2 had it.

  PRINT TI$

See also: @TIME$ @TI

= TRON
TRON

Traces the program: each line run goes to the editor's trace pane (or to the
log under Run alone).

  TRON

See also: @TROFF

= TROFF
TROFF

Ends TRON's trace.

  TROFF

See also: @TRON

= Events and the program model
A program runs its main lines, then waits for events: menu items, icons,
clicks, keys, the wheel, timers. Each event calls its SUB, one at a time, to
its end. The menus, the clock and the switcher keep working while BASIC
runs.

@WAIT @EVENTS @DOEVENTS @SLEEP @CLICK @KEY @WHEEL @QUIT @TIMER

= WAIT
WAIT EVENTS

Ends the main lines and waits for events. Running off the end of the main
lines does the same.

  WAIT EVENTS

See also: @DOEVENTS @ON @EVENTS

= EVENTS
WAIT EVENTS

The word of WAIT EVENTS.

  WAIT EVENTS

See also: @WAIT

= DOEVENTS
DOEVENTS

Inside a long loop: runs the handlers of the events waiting now, then
continues.

  FOR i% = 1 TO 1000 : DOEVENTS : NEXT

See also: @WAIT @SLEEP

= SLEEP
SLEEP frames

Waits that many frames (1/60 s); events are handled meanwhile.

  SLEEP 60

See also: @TIMER @DOEVENTS

= CLICK
ON CLICK CALL procedure

The event of a press of the left button outside the menus and icons, and of
its release; MOUSEB has bit 7 set on a release.

  ON CLICK CALL Clicked

See also: @ON @MOUSEB @MOUSEX

= KEY
ON KEY CALL procedure

The event of a key; the handler reads KEY$, KEYCODE and KEYMODS.

  ON KEY CALL Typed

See also: @ON @KEY$ @KEYCODE

= WHEEL
WHEEL
ON WHEEL CALL procedure

The wheel's movement since the last read (signed; reading clears it), and
the event of a wheel turn.

  ON WHEEL CALL Scrolled
  dy% = WHEEL

See also: @ON @MOUSEB

= QUIT
ON QUIT CALL procedure

The event of Quit from VeraBASIC Run's own menu while the program runs; its
SUB decides (END quits).

  ON QUIT CALL AskQuit

See also: @ON @APP

= TIMER
TIMER n, frames CALL procedure
TIMER n ON|OFF|STOP

Timer n (1-8) calls the SUB every so many frames. OFF pauses it, ON starts
it again, STOP ends it.

  TIMER 1, 60 CALL Tick
  TIMER 1 OFF

See also: @CALL @SLEEP @TI

= The application
APP lines describe the program for Make application: its name, class,
documents and tile. They do nothing when the program runs.

@APP @NAME @CLASS @VERSION @AUTHOR @DESCRIPTION @DOCUMENTS @STAY @CLASSIC

= APP
APP NAME n$ [, CLASS c$] [, VERSION v$] [, AUTHOR a$] | APP DESCRIPTION d$ |
    APP DOCUMENTS e$ | APP TILE colour | APP STAY | APP CLASSIC

The application's header (its app.hdr): name, GEOS class, version, author,
description, the file extensions it opens, its tile and flags.

  APP NAME "Bird Watch", CLASS "BirdWatch", VERSION "1.0", AUTHOR "Me"
  APP DOCUMENTS "BRD"

See also: @NAME @TILE @STAY @CLASSIC

= NAME
APP NAME n$
NAME old$ AS new$

In APP: the program's name. As a statement: renames a file.

  NAME "Draft.txt" AS "Final.txt"

See also: @APP @AS

= CLASS
APP ..., CLASS c$

The program's GEOS class (12 characters), which its documents name.

  APP NAME "Notes", CLASS "Notes"

See also: @APP

= VERSION
APP ..., VERSION v$

The program's version, "1.0" style.

  APP NAME "Notes", VERSION "1.2"

See also: @APP

= AUTHOR
APP ..., AUTHOR a$

Who wrote the program (19 characters).

  APP NAME "Notes", AUTHOR "Me"

See also: @APP

= DESCRIPTION
APP DESCRIPTION d$

The text the shell shows in the program's information (95 characters).

  APP DESCRIPTION "Counts the birds at the feeder."

See also: @APP

= DOCUMENTS
APP DOCUMENTS e$ [, e$] ...

Up to four file extensions the shell opens with the program.

  APP DOCUMENTS "BRD", "TXT"

See also: @APP @DOCUMENT$

= STAY
APP STAY

The program is never put aside; the switcher asks to quit it.

  APP STAY

See also: @APP @ASIDE

= CLASSIC
APP CLASSIC

The first menu is not named after the program (the Look 2 rule is not
applied).

  APP CLASSIC

See also: @MENU

= Windows
A program draws its own window chrome; up to 4 windows. After WINDOW USE,
drawing is relative to that window's body and clipped to it.

@WINDOW @TITLE @STATUS @TOOL @SHADOW @USE @BACK @FRONT @CLOSE @HIT @BODYTOP
@BODYBOTTOM @SCREEN @FULL

= WINDOW
WINDOW #w, x, y, wc, hc, title$ [, TOOL n] [, STATUS n] [, CLOSE] [, FRAME]
    [, SHADOW]
WINDOW TITLE|STATUS|USE|BACK|FRONT|CLOSE #w ...

Opens window w (1-4) at card x, y, wc cards wide and hc high, with a title
and options: a tool band and a status band of n rows, a close glyph, a
frame, a shadow. The second form changes it.

  WINDOW #1, 10, 6, 60, 40, "Bird Watch", CLOSE, FRAME, SHADOW, STATUS 2
  WINDOW TITLE #1, "Bird Watch - Garden"
  WINDOW USE #1

See also: @TITLE @STATUS @USE @CLOSE @HIT

= TITLE
WINDOW TITLE #w, title$

Changes a window's title. In an expression TITLE is the colour number 5 (the
title bars' colour) of PAPER.

  WINDOW TITLE #1, f$

See also: @WINDOW

= STATUS
WINDOW STATUS #w, left$, right$
WINDOW ..., STATUS rows

Writes a window's status band, left and right; as a WINDOW option, how many
rows it has.

  WINDOW STATUS #1, "12 birds", TIME$

See also: @WINDOW

= TOOL
WINDOW ..., TOOL rows

A tool band of that many rows under the title.

  WINDOW #1, 0, 2, 80, 56, "Sketch", TOOL 3

See also: @WINDOW

= SHADOW
WINDOW ..., SHADOW

A shadow right of and below the window.

  WINDOW #2, 20, 10, 30, 12, "About", FRAME, SHADOW

See also: @WINDOW @FRAME

= USE
WINDOW USE #w

Draws into window w: coordinates from its body's corner, clipped to the
body.

  WINDOW USE #1 : CLS : TEXT 4, 12, "Hello"

See also: @WINDOW @BODYTOP

= BACK
WINDOW BACK #w

Draws window w as not in front (a plain title band).

  WINDOW BACK #2

See also: @FRONT

= FRONT
WINDOW FRONT #w

Draws window w as the front one.

  WINDOW FRONT #1

See also: @BACK

= CLOSE
CLOSE [#h [, #h] ...]
WINDOW CLOSE #w
WINDOW ..., CLOSE

Closes files (all of them without a number). WINDOW CLOSE erases a window;
as a WINDOW option, a close glyph; ON CLOSE #w is its event.

  CLOSE #1
  WINDOW CLOSE #2

See also: @OPEN @WINDOW @ON

= HIT
HIT(#w, x, y)

Where a point is in window w: 0 outside, 1 title, 2 close glyph, 3 tool
band, 4 body, 5 status band.

  IF HIT(#1, MOUSEX, MOUSEY) = 1 THEN PRINT "title"

See also: @WINDOW @MOUSEX

= BODYTOP
BODYTOP(#w)

The first pixel row of window w's body.

  y% = BODYTOP(#1) + 4

See also: @BODYBOTTOM

= BODYBOTTOM
BODYBOTTOM(#w)

The last pixel row of window w's body.

  h% = BODYBOTTOM(#1) - BODYTOP(#1) + 1

See also: @BODYTOP

= SCREEN
SCREEN FULL [title$]

A full-screen program under the menu bar (as Paint), with the title in the
bar.

  SCREEN FULL "Sketch"

See also: @FULL @WINDOW

= FULL
SCREEN FULL

The word of SCREEN FULL.

  SCREEN FULL

See also: @SCREEN

= Menus
MENU lines build the menu bar; MENU SHOW puts it up. Each ITEM names the SUB
it calls.

@MENU @ITEM @CHECK @{END MENU} @SHOW @ENABLE

= MENU
MENU title$
MENU SHOW
MENU ENABLE menu$, item$, on

Starts a menu of the bar (its ITEMs follow, up to END MENU or the next
MENU). MENU SHOW builds and shows the bar; MENU ENABLE disables an item (0:
shown in parentheses) or enables it again (1).

  MENU "File" : ITEM "Open...", DoOpen, "O" : ITEM "Quit", DoQuit, "Q"
  MENU SHOW
  MENU ENABLE "Edit", "Cut", 0

See also: @ITEM @SHOW @{END MENU} @CHECK

= ITEM
ITEM text$ [, procedure [, key$]]

A menu item that calls the SUB, with a keyboard shortcut (Ctrl and the key,
shown at the menu's right). ITEM "-" is not an item: the next item starts a
group, under a dotted line.

  ITEM "About Notes", About
  ITEM "Save", DoSave, "S"

See also: @MENU

= CHECK
CHECK item$, on

Puts a check mark ("*") before a menu item (1) or takes it away (0).

  CHECK "Bold", 1

See also: @ITEM @MENU

= END MENU
END MENU

Ends a menu's ITEM lines (optional).

  MENU "Edit" : ITEM "Copy", DoCopy, "C" : END MENU

See also: @MENU

= SHOW
MENU SHOW

The word of MENU SHOW.

  MENU SHOW

See also: @MENU

= ENABLE
MENU ENABLE menu$, item$, on

The word of MENU ENABLE.

  MENU ENABLE "File", "Save", 1

See also: @MENU

= Dialogs
@MSGBOX @ASK @INPUTBOX$ @FILEOPEN$ @FILESAVE$ @DIALOG @{END DIALOG} @RESULT
@{DIALOG RESULT} @LABEL @FIELD @BUTTON @OK @CANCEL

= MSGBOX
MSGBOX text$ [, title$]

A dialog box with the text (CHR$(10) starts a new line), the title in bold,
and OK.

  MSGBOX "Saved."

See also: @ASK @INPUTBOX$

= ASK
ASK(question$)

A dialog box with Yes and No: 1 for Yes, 0 for No.

  IF ASK("Discard changes?") THEN END

See also: @MSGBOX

= INPUTBOX$
INPUTBOX$(prompt$ [, default$ [, length]])

A dialog box that asks for a line of text; "" on Cancel.

  n$ = INPUTBOX$("Your name:")

See also: @INPUT @ASK

= FILEOPEN$
FILEOPEN$(title$ [, extension$])

The Open dialog (long names, folders, a type filter): the name chosen, "" on
Cancel; its folder becomes the current one (CURDIR$).

  f$ = FILEOPEN$("Open a list", "TXT")

See also: @FILESAVE$ @CURDIR$ @OPEN

= FILESAVE$
FILESAVE$(title$, default$)

The Save dialog: a name of up to 63 characters, "" on Cancel.

  f$ = FILESAVE$("Save as", "Birds 2026.txt")

See also: @FILEOPEN$

= DIALOG
DIALOG x, y, w, h ... END DIALOG

A dialog box of your own at cards x, y, w by h: LABEL, FIELD and BUTTON
lines up to END DIALOG; DIALOG RESULT tells how it closed.

  DIALOG 20, 10, 30, 12
    LABEL 2, 2, "Size"
    FIELD 10, 2, s$, 3
    BUTTON OK : BUTTON CANCEL
  END DIALOG

See also: @LABEL @FIELD @BUTTON @{DIALOG RESULT}

= END DIALOG
END DIALOG

Ends a DIALOG block and shows the box until a button closes it.

  DIALOG 20, 10, 20, 6 : BUTTON OK : END DIALOG

See also: @DIALOG

= RESULT
DIALOG RESULT

The word of DIALOG RESULT.

  IF DIALOG RESULT = 1 THEN PRINT "OK"

See also: @{DIALOG RESULT}

= DIALOG RESULT
DIALOG RESULT

How the last DIALOG closed: 1 OK, 2 Cancel.

  IF DIALOG RESULT = 1 THEN size% = VAL(s$)

See also: @DIALOG @BUTTON

= LABEL
LABEL x, y, text$

In a DIALOG: text at cards x, y of the box.

  LABEL 2, 2, "Width:"

See also: @DIALOG @FIELD

= FIELD
FIELD x, y, string-variable, length

In a DIALOG: a text field that edits the variable.

  FIELD 10, 2, w$, 4

See also: @DIALOG @LABEL

= BUTTON
BUTTON OK | CANCEL

In a DIALOG: an OK or a Cancel button.

  BUTTON OK

See also: @DIALOG @OK @CANCEL

= OK
BUTTON OK

The OK button of a DIALOG.

  BUTTON OK

See also: @BUTTON

= CANCEL
BUTTON CANCEL

The Cancel button of a DIALOG.

  BUTTON CANCEL

See also: @BUTTON

= Icons, tiles and pictures
Pictures are strings in GEOS's compacted bitmap form, made by PICTURE$ from
a file or from DATA lines of "#" and ".".

@ICON @ICONS @OFF @CLEAR @TILE @BITMAP @PICTURE$ @PICTURE

= ICON
ICON id, x, y, picture$, procedure
ICON OFF id

An icon at pixel x, y that calls the SUB when clicked; ICON OFF takes it
away.

  ICON 1, 16, 40, brush$, PickBrush
  ICON OFF 1

See also: @ICONS @PICTURE$ @BITMAP

= ICONS
ICONS CLEAR

Takes all icons away.

  ICONS CLEAR

See also: @ICON

= OFF
ICON OFF id   TIMER n OFF   SPRITE n OFF   SOUND OFF

Turns a thing off: an icon, a timer, a sprite, the voices.

  SPRITE 2 OFF

See also: @ICON @TIMER @SPRITE @SOUND

= CLEAR
ICONS CLEAR

The word of ICONS CLEAR.

  ICONS CLEAR

See also: @ICONS

= TILE
TILE x, y, name$ [, colour]
APP TILE colour
TILE DATA row$, ...

Draws a program's tile at cards x, y (as the shell does). APP TILE and TILE
DATA give the program's own tile: a colour and 32 rows of 32 "#" and ".".

  TILE 2, 4, "Veranda Edit"
  APP TILE SKY

See also: @APP @ICON

= BITMAP
BITMAP x, y, picture$

Draws a picture at pixel x, y.

  BITMAP 100, 60, logo$

See also: @PICTURE$ @ICON

= PICTURE$
PICTURE$(file$)
PICTURE$(DATA)

A picture read from a file, or from the next DATA items: its width and
height, then rows of "#" (ink) and "." (paper).

  brush$ = PICTURE$(DATA)
  DATA 8, 2, "#..##..#", ".##..##."

See also: @BITMAP @ICON @SPRITE

= PICTURE
CLIPBOARD PICTURE x1, y1, x2, y2

The word of CLIPBOARD PICTURE.

  CLIPBOARD PICTURE 0, 0, 63, 63

See also: @CLIPBOARD

= Graphics
Coordinates are pixels at 640 x 480 (in the window's body after WINDOW USE).
The screen has one ink colour; colour comes from the paper of 8 x 8 cards
(PAPER).

@CLS @PATTERN @BOX @FRAME @INVERT @LINE @PLOT @POINT @COPY @SCROLL @POINTER

= CLS
CLS

Clears the window's body (or the screen) to paper.

  CLS

See also: @BOX @PAPER

= PATTERN
PATTERN n

The fill pattern of BOX (0-33, GEOS's patterns; 0 paper, 1 ink, the
default).

  PATTERN 2 : BOX 10, 10, 100, 50

See also: @BOX

= BOX
BOX x1, y1, x2, y2

A rectangle filled with the pattern.

  BOX 10, 10, 100, 50

See also: @FRAME @INVERT @PATTERN

= FRAME
FRAME x1, y1, x2, y2
WINDOW ..., FRAME

A rectangle's outline in ink; as a WINDOW option, a frame round the window.

  FRAME 10, 10, 100, 50

See also: @BOX @WINDOW

= INVERT
INVERT x1, y1, x2, y2

Turns a rectangle's ink and paper over.

  INVERT 0, 0, 79, 15

See also: @BOX

= LINE
LINE x1, y1, x2, y2 [, INK | ERASE]

A line in ink (or paper with ERASE). LINE INPUT is a different statement.

  LINE 0, 0, 639, 479
  LINE 10, 20, 30, 40, ERASE

See also: @PLOT @{LINE INPUT}

= PLOT
PLOT x, y [, ERASE]

One point in ink (or paper).

  PLOT MOUSEX, MOUSEY

See also: @POINT @LINE

= POINT
POINT(x, y)

1 when the point is ink, 0 when paper.

  IF POINT(x%, y%) THEN PRINT "ink"

See also: @PLOT

= COPY
COPY x, y, w, h TO x2, y2

Copies a rectangle of the screen, x and width in cards, y and height in
pixels.

  COPY 0, 16, 40, 100 TO 40, 16

See also: @SCROLL

= SCROLL
SCROLL x, w, top, bottom, dy

Moves the rows from top to bottom by dy pixels (x and width in cards).

  SCROLL 0, 80, 16, 479, -8

See also: @COPY

= POINTER
POINTER ARROW | BUSY | IBEAM | CROSS

The pointer's shape.

  POINTER BUSY

See also: @MOUSEX

= Text and fonts
@TEXT @FONT @STYLE @TEXTWIDTH @FONTHEIGHT @CARDS

= TEXT
TEXT x, y, s$

Draws s$ in the current font and style, y the baseline.

  TEXT 20, 30, "Hello"

See also: @FONT @STYLE @CARDS

= FONT
FONT name$, size
FONT UI
FONT SYSTEM

The font of TEXT: one from the fonts folder, the UI font or the system font.

  FONT "Roma", 12
  FONT UI

See also: @TEXT @FONTHEIGHT

= STYLE
STYLE style

The style of TEXT: 0 plain, or BOLD, ITALIC, UNDERLINE, OUTLINE, REVERSE
added together.

  STYLE BOLD + ITALIC

See also: @TEXT

= TEXTWIDTH
TEXTWIDTH(s$)

The width in pixels of s$ in the current font and style.

  x% = 320 - TEXTWIDTH(t$) \ 2

See also: @FONTHEIGHT

= FONTHEIGHT
FONTHEIGHT

The height in pixels of the current font.

  y% = y% + FONTHEIGHT

See also: @TEXTWIDTH

= CARDS
CARDS column, row, s$ [, REVERSE | BOLD]

Fast text in the ROM's 8 x 8 font on the card grid (80 x 60).

  CARDS 0, 2, "Score: " + STR$(s%), BOLD

See also: @TEXT @PAPER

= Colour
VERA at 1 bit a pixel has one ink for the whole screen and a paper colour
for each 8 x 8 card. Colour means PAPER areas in the colours of Veranda's
look.

@PAPER @CARDCOLOR @INK @PALETTE

= PAPER
PAPER column, row, w, h, colour

Sets the paper colour of a rectangle of cards. In an expression PAPER is the
colour number 0 (the paper).

  PAPER 0, 2, 80, 1, YELLOW

See also: @CARDCOLOR @INK

= CARDCOLOR
CARDCOLOR(column, row)

The paper colour of a card.

  IF CARDCOLOR(5, 5) = RED THEN PRINT "red"

See also: @PAPER

= INK
INK rgb
LINE ..., INK

The screen's one ink colour as $RGB. In an expression INK is the number 1
(the ink: a drawing mode, or the colour number).

  INK $223

See also: @PALETTE @PAPER

= PALETTE
PALETTE n, rgb

Sets palette entry n (12-15 only; 0-11 are the look's) to $RGB.

  PALETTE 12, $F80

See also: @INK

= Mouse and keyboard
@MOUSEX @MOUSEY @MOUSEB @MOUSEIN @MOUSE @LIMIT @INKEY$ @KEY$ @KEYCODE
@KEYMODS

= MOUSEX
MOUSEX

The pointer's x (0-639).

  PLOT MOUSEX, MOUSEY

See also: @MOUSEY @MOUSEB

= MOUSEY
MOUSEY

The pointer's y (0-479).

  PRINT MOUSEX; MOUSEY

See also: @MOUSEX

= MOUSEB
MOUSEB

The buttons: bit 0 left, 1 right, 2 middle; in a click handler bit 7 set on
a release.

  IF MOUSEB AND 2 THEN PRINT "right"

See also: @CLICK @MOUSEX

= MOUSEIN
MOUSEIN(x1, y1, x2, y2)

1 when the pointer is in the rectangle.

  IF MOUSEIN(0, 0, 99, 99) THEN PRINT "corner"

See also: @MOUSEX @HIT

= MOUSE
MOUSE LIMIT x1, y1, x2, y2

Keeps the pointer inside the rectangle.

  MOUSE LIMIT 0, 16, 639, 479

See also: @LIMIT

= LIMIT
MOUSE LIMIT x1, y1, x2, y2

The word of MOUSE LIMIT.

  MOUSE LIMIT 100, 100, 539, 379

See also: @MOUSE

= INKEY$
INKEY$

The next key from the queue as a string, "" when none; never waits.

  k$ = INKEY$ : IF k$ <> "" THEN PRINT k$

See also: @GET @KEY$ @KEYCODE

= KEY$
KEY$

In a key handler: the key as a string.

  SUB Typed : PRINT KEY$; : END SUB

See also: @KEY @KEYCODE

= KEYCODE
KEYCODE

In a key handler: the key's code (compare with KEY_LEFT ...).

  IF KEYCODE = KEY_LEFT THEN x% = x% - 8

See also: @KEY$ @KEYMODS

= KEYMODS
KEYMODS

The modifier keys: Shift 1, Ctrl 2, Alt 4, GUI 8.

  IF KEYMODS AND 2 THEN PRINT "Ctrl"

See also: @KEYCODE

= Sound
@SOUND @BEEP @VOICE @PLAY

= SOUND
SOUND id
SOUND OFF

Plays a system sound (SND_ERROR ...; silent when the settings say so). SOUND
OFF silences all the program's voices.

  SOUND SND_ERROR
  SOUND OFF

See also: @BEEP @VOICE @PLAY

= BEEP
BEEP

The system beep.

  IF n% < 0 THEN BEEP

See also: @SOUND

= VOICE
VOICE v, frequency, volume [, wave [, width]]

Sets PSG voice v (0-13) directly: frequency in Hz, volume 0-63, wave PULSE,
SAWTOOTH, TRIANGLE or NOISE, the pulse width 0-63.

  VOICE 0, 440, 63, PULSE
  VOICE 0, 0, 0

See also: @PLAY @SOUND

= PLAY
PLAY v, music$

Plays a tune on voice v in the background: T tempo, O octave, notes C D E F
G A B with # and a length, R a rest.

  PLAY 0, "T120 O4 C8 E8 G4"

See also: @VOICE

= Files and folders
Files have long names (up to 63 characters, no comma); at most 3 are open. A
kernel's error becomes a catchable error (50 File not found, 51 Too many
files ...) with ERRFILE the kernel's code.

@OPEN @PUT @SEEK @LOF @LOC @EOF @KILL @AS @MKDIR @RMDIR @CHDIR @CURDIR$
@DIR$ @FOLDER$ @DOCUMENT$ @CLIPBOARD$ @CLIPBOARD

= OPEN
OPEN #h, name$, mode$

Opens a file as handle h (1-255; 3 files open at most): mode "r" read, "w"
write (a new file, or the old one replaced), "m" read and write (the file
must be there). A name may hold a folder ("Lists/Birds.txt"), 64 characters
in all.

  OPEN #1, "Holiday photos from the lake.txt", "w"

See also: @CLOSE @PRINT @INPUT @EOF

= PUT
PUT #h, s$

Writes the bytes of s$ to file h, nothing added.

  PUT #1, CHR$(0) + CHR$(1)

See also: @GET @PRINT

= SEEK
SEEK #h, position

Moves file h's position (0 is the first byte).

  SEEK #1, 0

See also: @LOC @LOF

= LOF
LOF(h)

The size of file h in bytes (up to 16 MB: a float above 32767).

  PRINT LOF(1); "bytes"

See also: @LOC @EOF

= LOC
LOC(h)

The position of file h.

  p = LOC(1)

See also: @SEEK @LOF

= EOF
EOF(h)

True (-1) at the end of file h.

  WHILE NOT EOF(1) : LINE INPUT #1, l$ : PRINT l$ : WEND

See also: @{LINE INPUT} @LOF

= KILL
KILL name$

Deletes a file.

  KILL "Old notes.txt"

See also: @NAME

= AS
NAME old$ AS new$

The word of NAME ... AS.

  NAME a$ AS b$

See also: @NAME

= MKDIR
MKDIR path$

Makes a folder.

  MKDIR "Birds"

See also: @RMDIR @CHDIR

= RMDIR
RMDIR path$

Removes an empty folder.

  RMDIR "Birds"

See also: @MKDIR

= CHDIR
CHDIR path$

Makes a folder the current one.

  CHDIR FOLDER$(DOCS)

See also: @CURDIR$ @FOLDER$

= CURDIR$
CURDIR$

The current folder's path.

  PRINT CURDIR$

See also: @CHDIR

= DIR$
DIR$(pattern$)   then   DIR$()

The first name in the current folder matching the pattern ("*" any run, "?"
any one, either case), then with DIR$() the next ones; "" when there are no
more. A folder's name ends with "/".

  f$ = DIR$("*.TXT") : WHILE f$ <> "" : PRINT f$ : f$ = DIR$() : WEND

See also: @CHDIR @KILL

= FOLDER$
FOLDER$(DOCS | APPS | FONTS | SYSTEM | DATA)

The path of one of the card's folders.

  CHDIR FOLDER$(DOCS)

See also: @CHDIR

= DOCUMENT$
DOCUMENT$

The document a made application was opened with (its long name), "" if none;
under VeraBASIC Run always "".

  IF DOCUMENT$ <> "" THEN OPEN #1, DOCUMENT$, "r"

See also: @DOCUMENTS @FILEOPEN$

= CLIPBOARD$
CLIPBOARD$

The clipboard's text (the Text Scrap, plain, 255 characters at most); a line
ends with CHR$(10).

  t$ = CLIPBOARD$

See also: @CLIPBOARD

= CLIPBOARD
CLIPBOARD s$
CLIPBOARD PICTURE x1, y1, x2, y2

Puts text on the clipboard (the Text Scrap), or a rectangle of the screen
(the Photo Scrap).

  CLIPBOARD "copied text"

See also: @CLIPBOARD$ @PICTURE

= Time, sprites and the switcher
@TIME$ @DATE$ @SPRITE @AT @ASIDE @TASKS @TASK$

= TIME$
TIME$

The time as "HH:MM:SS".

  WINDOW STATUS #1, "", TIME$

See also: @DATE$ @TI$

= DATE$
DATE$

The date as "YYYY-MM-DD".

  PRINT DATE$

See also: @TIME$

= SPRITE
SPRITE n, picture$
SPRITE n AT x, y
SPRITE n ON|OFF

Sprites 2-7 (0 is the pointer, 1 the text cursor): a 24 x 21 picture, a
place, shown or hidden.

  SPRITE 2, ball$ : SPRITE 2 AT 100, 100 : SPRITE 2 ON

See also: @AT @PICTURE$

= AT
SPRITE n AT x, y

The word of SPRITE ... AT.

  SPRITE 3 AT x%, y%

See also: @SPRITE

= ASIDE
ASIDE

Puts the program aside (as Alt+Tab does) and shows the dock; it continues
when brought back.

  ASIDE

See also: @TASKS @STAY

= TASKS
TASKS

How many programs are put aside.

  FOR i% = 1 TO TASKS : PRINT TASK$(i%) : NEXT

See also: @TASK$ @ASIDE

= TASK$
TASK$(i)

The name of the i-th program put aside.

  PRINT TASK$(1)

See also: @TASKS

= Constants
Predefined names for numbers. They are names, not keywords: a program may
not assign them, and a name of its own may not take their spelling.

@CHROME @SIDEBAR @YELLOW @RED @GREEN @PURPLE @ORANGE @CYAN @SKY @SKY2 @HILL
@DUSK @ARROW @BUSY @IBEAM @CROSS @ERASE @BOLD @ITALIC @UNDERLINE @OUTLINE
@REVERSE @PULSE @SAWTOOTH @TRIANGLE @NOISE @DOCS @APPS @FONTS @SYSTEM @UI
@KEY_ @SND_

= CHROME
CHROME

The colour of bars and buttons (2).

Value: 2

  PAPER 0, 0, 80, 2, CHROME

See also: @PAPER

= SIDEBAR
SIDEBAR

The sidebar colour (3).

Value: 3

  PAPER 0, 2, 20, 58, SIDEBAR

See also: @PAPER

= YELLOW
YELLOW

Yellow paper (6).

Value: 6

  PAPER 0, 2, 80, 1, YELLOW

See also: @PAPER

= RED
RED

Red paper (7).

Value: 7

  PAPER 10, 10, 5, 1, RED

See also: @PAPER

= GREEN
GREEN

Green paper (8).

Value: 8

  PAPER 10, 11, 5, 1, GREEN

See also: @PAPER

= PURPLE
PURPLE

Purple paper (9).

Value: 9

  PAPER 10, 12, 5, 1, PURPLE

See also: @PAPER

= ORANGE
ORANGE

Orange paper (10).

Value: 10

  PAPER 10, 13, 5, 1, ORANGE

See also: @PAPER

= CYAN
CYAN

Cyan paper (11), the Veranda mark's colour.

Value: 11

  PAPER 10, 14, 5, 1, CYAN

See also: @PAPER

= SKY
SKY

Sky blue (12); palette entry 12 (PALETTE may change it).

Value: 12

  APP TILE SKY

See also: @PAPER @PALETTE

= SKY2
SKY2

Light sky blue (13); palette entry 13.

Value: 13

  PAPER 0, 40, 80, 4, SKY2

See also: @PAPER

= HILL
HILL

Palette entry 14: Sunroom's sage, the desktop's colour (before Look 3 a
darker hill green). For a hill or grass, GREEN.

Value: 14

  PAPER 10, 15, 5, 1, HILL

See also: @PAPER @GREEN

= DUSK
DUSK

Dusk pink (15); palette entry 15.

Value: 15

  PAPER 0, 0, 80, 10, DUSK

See also: @PAPER

= ARROW
POINTER ARROW

The arrow pointer.

Value: 0

  POINTER ARROW

See also: @POINTER

= BUSY
POINTER BUSY

The busy pointer (animated).

Value: 1

  POINTER BUSY

See also: @POINTER

= IBEAM
POINTER IBEAM

The I-beam pointer, for text.

Value: 2

  POINTER IBEAM

See also: @POINTER

= CROSS
POINTER CROSS

The cross-hair pointer.

Value: 3

  POINTER CROSS

See also: @POINTER

= ERASE
LINE ..., ERASE   PLOT x, y, ERASE

Draws in paper instead of ink.

Value: 0

  LINE 0, 0, 100, 100, ERASE

See also: @LINE @PLOT @INK

= BOLD
STYLE BOLD   CARDS ..., BOLD

Bold text.

Value: 64

  STYLE BOLD

See also: @STYLE @CARDS

= ITALIC
STYLE ITALIC

Italic text.

Value: 16

  STYLE ITALIC

See also: @STYLE

= UNDERLINE
STYLE UNDERLINE

Underlined text.

Value: 128

  STYLE UNDERLINE

See also: @STYLE

= OUTLINE
STYLE OUTLINE

Outlined text.

Value: 8

  STYLE OUTLINE

See also: @STYLE

= REVERSE
STYLE REVERSE   CARDS ..., REVERSE

Text in paper on ink.

Value: 32

  CARDS 0, 0, " Menu ", REVERSE

See also: @STYLE @CARDS

= PULSE
VOICE v, f, vol, PULSE

The pulse wave (with a width).

Value: 0

  VOICE 0, 440, 63, PULSE, 32

See also: @VOICE

= SAWTOOTH
VOICE v, f, vol, SAWTOOTH

The sawtooth wave.

Value: 1

  VOICE 1, 220, 40, SAWTOOTH

See also: @VOICE

= TRIANGLE
VOICE v, f, vol, TRIANGLE

The triangle wave.

Value: 2

  VOICE 2, 330, 50, TRIANGLE

See also: @VOICE

= NOISE
VOICE v, f, vol, NOISE

Noise.

Value: 3

  VOICE 3, 1000, 30, NOISE

See also: @VOICE

= DOCS
FOLDER$(DOCS)

The user's documents folder (/DOCUMENTS).

Value: 1

  CHDIR FOLDER$(DOCS)

See also: @FOLDER$

= APPS
FOLDER$(APPS)

The applications folder (/VERANDA/APPS).

Value: 2

  PRINT FOLDER$(APPS)

See also: @FOLDER$

= FONTS
FOLDER$(FONTS)

The fonts folder (/VERANDA/FONTS).

Value: 3

  PRINT FOLDER$(FONTS)

See also: @FOLDER$

= SYSTEM
FOLDER$(SYSTEM)   FONT SYSTEM

The system folder (/VERANDA); with FONT, the system font.

Value: 4

  FONT SYSTEM

See also: @FOLDER$ @FONT

= UI
FONT UI

The UI font (the menus' and dialogs').

Value: 6

  FONT UI

See also: @FONT

= KEY_
KEY_LEFT, KEY_RIGHT, KEY_UP, KEY_DOWN, KEY_F1 ... (api/veranda.api)

Key codes, as KEYCODE gives them; the names and numbers are the kernel's
(api/veranda.api).

KEY_F1             1  key code
KEY_F2             2  key code
KEY_F3             3  key code
KEY_F4             4  key code
KEY_F5             5  key code
KEY_F6             6  key code
KEY_LEFT           8  key code (BACKSPACE)
KEY_TAB            9  key code
KEY_F7            14  key code
KEY_F8            15  key code
KEY_UP            16  key code
KEY_DOWN          17  key code
KEY_HOME          18  key code
KEY_CLEAR         19  key code
KEY_STOP          22  key code
KEY_ESC           27  key code
KEY_INSERT        28  key code
KEY_DELETE        29  key code
KEY_RIGHT         30  key code
KEY_PGUP         129  key code (API 2)
KEY_PGDN         130  key code (API 2; without Ctrl: Ctrl+B is 2)
KEY_END          131  key code (API 2; without Ctrl: Ctrl+D is 4)
KEY_F9           137  key code (API 2)
KEY_F10          138  key code (API 2)
KEY_F11          139  key code (API 2)
KEY_F12          140  key code (API 2)

  IF KEYCODE = KEY_ESC THEN END

See also: @KEYCODE @KEY

= SND_
SOUND SND_ERROR ... (api/veranda.api)

The system sounds of SOUND; the names and numbers are the kernel's
(api/veranda.api).

SND_ERROR          1  PlaySound: an error (Beep)
SND_WARNING        2  PlaySound: a warning or a question (a dialog box with
                      YES and NO plays it)
SND_ALARM          3  PlaySound: the alarm (plays with the sounds off;
                      nothing cuts it)
SND_STARTUP        4  PlaySound: the start-up chime (the first program after
                      boot)
SND_EXIT           5  PlaySound: leaving Veranda (ToBASIC plays it to its
                      end)
SND_APP            6  PlaySound: an application starts
SND_OPEN           7  PlaySound: a desk accessory opens (or a window)
SND_CLOSE          8  PlaySound: a desk accessory closes (or a window)
SND_MENU           9  PlaySound: a menu tick (only with PREFS_MENUTICK)
SND_TRASH         10  PlaySound: the trash emptied

  SOUND SND_WARNING

See also: @SOUND
